    <div class="lp-hero">
      <div><p class="lp-kicker">The CallTeam buying desk / 07</p><h1>LinkedIn Sales Navigator <span>vs Apollo.</span><small>For lead generation.</small></h1><p class="lp-intro">Which one helps your team find the right buyers and reach them? Compare the cost, contact access and work each leaves you to do.</p><div class="lp-actions"><a class="lp-button" href="#comparison">Compare Sales Navigator and Apollo ↓</a><a class="lp-text-link" href="#prospect-brief">Get the free prospect brief →</a></div></div>
      <aside class="lp-brief" aria-labelledby="sn-brief-title"><p class="lp-kicker">Before buying another subscription</p><h2 id="sn-brief-title">What is stopping<br>your next conversation?</h2><ol><li><span>01</span><div><strong>You cannot identify the buyer.</strong><p>Find who owns the buying decision.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><strong>You are missing a useful number.</strong><p>Test a contact-data provider.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><strong>Your list sits untouched.</strong><p>Assign the calling and follow-up.</p></div></li></ol><a href="#generate-leads">Follow a prospect through to the first call →</a></aside>
    </div>
    <p class="lp-direct-answer"><strong>CallTeam verdict:</strong> Sales Navigator is a weak first purchase when your main problem is missing phone numbers. We would test a contact provider first. Pay for Sales Navigator when deeper account research improves who you approach and why.</p>
    <p class="lp-method"><strong>Sources checked {{ guide.reviewed }}.</strong> Official sources are linked in the reviews.</p>
  </div></div>
