  <section class="lp-section" id="worth-it"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">01 / What you actually get</p><h2>What is Sales Navigator<br>good for?</h2><p>Sales Navigator is LinkedIn's paid sales prospecting product. It helps you search for people and companies, track accounts and approach members. It can support lead generation when your team has a clear outreach process.</p></div>
    <div class="sn-two"><div><h3>Useful research before you call.</h3><ul><li><strong>Finding the buying role.</strong> Search within suitable accounts and inspect the person's responsibilities. This helps when a generic title search produces too many weak matches.</li><li><strong>Following relevant changes.</strong> A new role or company update can prompt a useful question. Read the underlying information before building a pitch around it.</li><li><strong>Finding a way into an account.</strong> Shared connections and account context can help you approach people with a relevant introduction.</li></ul><p class="lp-sources"><a href="https://business.linkedin.com/sell/sales-navigator/how-to-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn's search, account and outreach guidance ↗</a></p></div><div><h3>The work it leaves you.</h3><p>A profile needs to be checked against your offer. A saved name still needs a contact route, a message and follow-up. InMail gives you another way to approach someone; it does not guarantee they will respond.</p><p>The cost also includes the time spent researching. Give that work a stopping point. Once you know why the company fits, who to approach and what to ask, prepare the outreach.</p><p><strong>If your reps spend the week saving profiles and cannot show qualified conversations or held meetings, review the workflow before renewing.</strong></p></div></div>
    <div class="lp-note"><strong>Start with ordinary LinkedIn if the research is occasional.</strong><p>If you already have a short target list, check whether the information you can see with your current account is enough. Upgrade when a specific search or account feature saves useful work. A paid seat alone will not fix unclear targeting.</p></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="comparison"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">02 / Choose around the job</p><h2>Sales Navigator vs Apollo:<br>what changes for your team?</h2><p>Apollo combines company and contact data with outreach software. Sales Navigator builds on LinkedIn's professional network. Both can help you research prospects; compare how you will reach and work those contacts.</p></div>
    <div class="sn-table-wrap"><table class="lp-table sn-table" role="table"><caption>Sales Navigator and Apollo compared for a B2B prospecting workflow</caption><thead><tr role="row"><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Your job</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">LinkedIn Sales Navigator</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Apollo</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">CallTeam verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for row in guide.comparison %}<tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">{{ row.job }}</th><td role="cell" data-label="Sales Navigator">{{ row.navigator }}</td><td role="cell" data-label="Apollo">{{ row.apollo }}</td><td role="cell" data-label="CallTeam verdict">{{ row.verdict }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
    <p class="lp-sources sn-sources"><a href="https://business.linkedin.com/sales-solutions/learning-center/resources/tip-sheets/ts036" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sales Navigator lead information ↗</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/help/sales-navigator/answer/a102031" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn's export and CRM limits ↗</a><a href="https://www.apollo.io/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo plans and workflow limits ↗</a></p>
    <div class="sn-two sn-after"><div><h3>When using both makes sense.</h3><p>Sales Navigator helps you identify the right person or understand an account. Apollo then supplies a missing contact route or supports the outreach. Check that each subscription adds something your team uses.</p><p>Match the person by name, current company and role before buying contact details. Check the company website or domain as well. Similar names and old job records can send you to the wrong buyer.</p></div><div><h3>When we would skip a second tool.</h3><p>Your current provider covers the target accounts and gives your callers enough context. The real backlog is unworked leads or missed follow-up. Fix that first.</p><p>If Apollo's phone coverage is the problem, compare a small batch from another data source. Our <a href="/resources/apollo-alternatives/">Apollo alternatives guide</a> covers that decision. Sales Navigator should earn its fee through useful research.</p></div></div>
    <div class="sn-mid-cta"><div><h3>Already have a prospect list?</h3><p>Hire CallTeam to prepare the calling list, contact the buyers and handle follow-up. Bring the data you are authorised to use.</p></div><a class="lp-button" href="https://calendly.com/hello-callteam/new-meeting" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-booking-placement="sales-navigator-comparison">Book a free 30-minute call ↗</a></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section" id="pricing"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">03 / Understand the bill</p><h2>Sales Navigator pricing<br>compared with Apollo.</h2><p>All amounts in this table are <strong>USD per licence or seat, before tax and extras</strong>. An annual monthly equivalent requires the annual purchase. Confirm the final offer at checkout.</p></div>
    <div class="sn-table-wrap"><table class="lp-table sn-table sn-price-table" role="table"><caption>Published subscription references checked 8 November 2026</caption><thead><tr role="row"><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Plan</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Annual purchase</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Monthly billing</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Check before paying</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">Sales Navigator Core</th><td role="cell" data-label="Annual purchase"><strong>USD 1,079.88/year</strong><span>USD 89.99/month equivalent.</span></td><td role="cell" data-label="Monthly billing"><strong>USD 119.99/month</strong></td><td role="cell" data-label="Check before paying">50 InMails/month. Check contact coverage and CRM access before buying.</td></tr>
      <tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">Sales Navigator Advanced</th><td role="cell" data-label="Annual purchase"><strong>USD 1,799.88/year</strong><span>USD 149.99/month equivalent.</span></td><td role="cell" data-label="Monthly billing"><strong>USD 159.99/month</strong></td><td role="cell" data-label="Check before paying">Also 50 InMails/month. Buy the team features only if the team will use them.</td></tr>
      <tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">Sales Navigator Advanced Plus</th><td role="cell" data-label="Annual purchase"><strong>Custom quote</strong></td><td role="cell" data-label="Monthly billing">Ask for the available term.</td><td role="cell" data-label="Check before paying">Supported CRM Sync and lead creation. Confirm compatibility and the full contract.</td></tr>
      <tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">Apollo Basic</th><td role="cell" data-label="Annual purchase"><strong>USD 588/year</strong><span>USD 49/seat/month equivalent.</span></td><td role="cell" data-label="Monthly billing">Confirm current checkout rate.</td><td role="cell" data-label="Check before paying">Published annual allowance: 30,000 credits per seat. Phone requests and other operations consume the allowance.</td></tr>
      <tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">Apollo Professional</th><td role="cell" data-label="Annual purchase"><strong>USD 948/year</strong><span>USD 79/seat/month equivalent.</span></td><td role="cell" data-label="Monthly billing">Confirm current checkout rate.</td><td role="cell" data-label="Check before paying">Published annual allowance: 48,000 credits per seat. Confirm the calling options, usage and add-ons you need.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="lp-small">For Canada, LinkedIn lists Core from <strong>CAD 144.99/month or CAD 1,199.88/year</strong>. Apollo figures above stay in USD. Apollo's annual figures come from its published guide. We have not verified its current monthly checkout price.</p>
    <p class="lp-sources sn-sources"><a href="https://business.linkedin.com/sell/sales-navigator/compare-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn's prices and plan comparison ↗</a><a href="https://www.apollo.io/insights/billing-prospects-and-leads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo's published annual prices and credits ↗</a><a href="https://knowledge.apollo.io/hc/en-us/articles/48632642150285-Estimate-Your-Apollo-Costs-with-the-Pricing-Calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo's checkout and add-on guidance ↗</a></p>
    <div class="sn-two sn-after"><div><h3>Phone credits need their own budget.</h3><p>Apollo publishes an <strong>eight-credit phone lookup</strong> in its current credit guidance. Other lookups, enrichment and calling can consume credits too. Check the deduction for your chosen action and plan before running a batch.</p><p>Apollo distinguishes mobiles, work-direct, home and other personal numbers. Keep the returned type attached to the record. A paid lookup does not establish that you have a current mobile for the intended buyer.</p><p class="lp-sources"><a href="https://www.apollo.io/insights/how-do-i-build-a-prospect-list-on-apollos-free-plan-without-burning-credits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo's published lookup costs ↗</a> · <a href="https://knowledge.apollo.io/hc/en-us/articles/31969477982221-Access-a-Prospect-s-Phone-Number" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phone types and HQ-number access ↗</a></p></div><div><h3>Check the path out of Sales Navigator.</h3><p>LinkedIn does not offer native CSV or XLS export of lead and account information. Advanced Plus supports specific CRM routes. Confirm that the route you need exists on the plan you are buying.</p><p>Contact information can appear on a lead page, including details from a profile or connected CRM. Check its source. Buying Sales Navigator does not guarantee complete contact coverage for your list.</p><p>For contact-provider choices, use our <a href="/resources/b2b-lead-providers/">lead generation database comparison</a>. Teams reviewing a larger data contract can use the <a href="/resources/zoominfo-alternatives/">ZoomInfo alternatives guide</a>.</p></div></div>
    <div class="lp-note"><strong>CallTeam verdict: pay for the missing capability.</strong><p>Sales Navigator is hard to justify if its research rarely changes your outreach. Apollo is poor value if paid contacts do not fit your market or nobody works them. Test useful coverage and the team's follow-through before adding seats.</p></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="generate-leads"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">04 / Put the research to work</p><h2>How to generate leads<br>with Sales Navigator.</h2><p>Use the research to prepare a relevant approach. Here is the process we would give a small sales team.</p><p>Planning automated follow-ups? Compare <a href="/resources/best-linkedin-automation-tools/">LinkedIn automation tools, sender costs and reply handling</a> before choosing the software.</p></div>
    <ol class="sn-steps">
      <li><h3>Define the accounts worth calling.</h3><p>Choose the territory, company type, size and problem your offer addresses. Write down exclusions. Start with a manageable batch so you can inspect the results and follow up properly.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Find the person who owns the issue.</h3><p>Search relevant roles inside those accounts. Check responsibilities and location. A senior title can still belong to someone with no authority over your offer.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Find a business reason to approach them.</h3><p>Use a company update, an operating need or a clear fit with your service. Save the source. A job change gives you something to investigate; ask about the situation before assuming there is a budget.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Obtain a usable contact route.</h3><p>Check existing records first. Fill a missing phone or email through a suitable source, then confirm the person and company match. Keep stop requests out of the outreach queue. Our <a href="/resources/how-to-buy-cold-calling-leads/">mobile vs company numbers guide</a> helps you choose what to call.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Prepare the first question and call.</h3><p>Introduce yourself, give a short reason for calling and ask about the issue. Keep the research relevant to the business. Log a wrong number separately from a call that went unanswered.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Make the next action clear.</h3><p>Record the outcome, callback time and owner. Send useful follow-up when appropriate. Use a <a href="/resources/best-crm-for-outbound-sales/">CRM that keeps the calling work organised</a>; compare <a href="/resources/best-cold-calling-dialers/">diallers</a> when the calling setup becomes a bottleneck.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="sn-example"><p class="lp-kicker">Illustrative example / commercial cleaning</p><h3>Turn a company search into a relevant call.</h3><p class="lp-small">Worked example: a commercial cleaning company prospecting in its service area.</p><dl><dt>Company search</dt><dd>Office-based businesses in the territory you can serve. Exclude sites outside your service area and businesses that do not fit your contract size.</dd><dt>Buying-role search</dt><dd>Facilities, office management or operations within those accounts. Check who manages cleaning for that location.</dd><dt>Reason to approach</dt><dd>A confirmed office move, or a straightforward question about how the current cleaning arrangement is reviewed.</dd><dt>Contact handoff</dt><dd>Buyer name, location, source, suitable number and the question the caller will ask.</dd></dl><p class="sn-opening">“Hi, it's Alex with [company]. We handle commercial cleaning in [area]. Who looks after the cleaning contract for your office?”</p></div>
    <p class="lp-small">If you have confirmed the right person, ask them directly: “How are you reviewing the cleaning arrangement this year?” Adapt the question to what your business actually provides.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section" id="calling-email-linkedin"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">05 / CallTeam's approach</p><h2>Where calling, email<br>and LinkedIn fit.</h2><p>We build outreach around calling suitable prospects. A conversation lets the caller check responsibility, understand the objection and agree on a next step while the buyer is there.</p></div>
    <div class="sn-two"><div><h3>Call when the account and number fit.</h3><p>Expect unanswered calls, voicemail and screening. A useful calling process includes retry decisions, company-number fallbacks and callbacks. The caller still has to earn the conversation.</p><p>Use email to send the detail someone requested, support a call or follow up with a relevant reason. Keep messages tied to the buyer's situation.</p></div><div><h3>Give LinkedIn a specific job.</h3><p>Use it to check the buyer, understand the account or find an introduction. Message people when you have a relevant approach and evidence that the channel works for your market.</p><p>Cut back on activity that consumes time without producing useful conversations. Track the channels that helped a meeting happen, including research and earlier touches.</p></div></div>
    <div class="sn-evidence"><h3>What the published calling research shows.</h3><div class="sn-stat-pair" aria-label="Email reply rates reported by Gong"><div><strong>1.81%</strong><span>Email replies without cold calls</span></div><div><strong>3.44%</strong><span>Email replies with cold calls</span></div></div><p>Gong reports these email reply rates in its guide based on more than 300 million calls recorded on its platform. The figures support testing calls alongside email. These are observed reply rates. The report does not rank phone, email and LinkedIn across all markets.</p><p class="lp-sources"><a href="https://www.gong.io/files/gong-guide-how-to-master-cold-calls.pdf#page=2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read Gong's report and definitions, page 2 ↗</a></p></div>
    <div class="lp-note"><strong>Review outcomes before renewing.</strong><p>For a small batch, record research time, suitable contacts with usable numbers, qualified conversations and held meetings. Keep the offer, caller and follow-up approach consistent enough to interpret the results. Use the findings to decide what to keep, improve or cancel.</p></div>
  </div></section>

